---
// Lesson 7.6a — Level Studio I
// Build day: paper to playable. First half of the big cave.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.6a — Level Studio I</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Paper day is over (<a href="7.5a.html">7.5a</a>); build day is
    here. Today the sketch becomes tiles, beats become rooms, and by
    the end of class your cave is WALKABLE from entrance to the boss
    door — unpolished, ugly, and real.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Studio Brief</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The contract:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>All 6 beats of your sketch exist as tiles — entrance to
            boss door, no gaps</li>
        <li>Every beat is <em>passable</em> (you do not need it
            FUN yet — you need it REACHABLE)</li>
        <li>Enemies/turrets/spikes placed ONLY where the sketch says —
            resist the urge to sprinkle (sprinkling is what makes
            levels random; placement is what makes them fair)</li>
        <li>Ladders work top AND bottom
            (<a href="7.4a.html">7.4a</a> solutions applied)</li>
        <li>Soft gate present if your design has one</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Build order (it matters):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li>Grey-box the FLOOR of the whole cave first — one long
            walk from entrance to boss door, flat. Run it. The walk
            is the spine; everything else hangs off it</li>
        <li>Add the vertical structure (stairs, walls, ladders) beat
            by beat. Run between each beat — walk the whole cave
            every time. The 60-second full walk is your build
            ritual</li>
        <li>Place the threats LAST, from the sketch, with their
            numbers already chosen (patrol lengths, cooldowns,
            spike rows). Threats are the most expensive thing to
            move, so they go in when geometry is stable</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Studio smells</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You cannot make beat 3's jump without the boots. Is
                that a gate or a mistake? What distinguishes them —
                a VISIBLE alternative? Mark your sketch.</li>
            <li>Your walk ritual discovers a seam stop
                (<a href="7.2a.html">7.2a</a> invisible wall) in
                beat 2. Why is finding it NOW cheap and finding it
                during a playtest expensive?</li>
            <li>An enemy fell through the floor into the void. What
                did you forget at its spawn point — and why do
                placed-from-prefab enemies remind you about
                colliders the hard way?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Checkpoint</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>End-of-studio checks — every one answers to the walk ritual:</p>
    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Close the day</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Walk the full cave, out loud, naming each beat as you
                enter it ("teeth... rest..."). If you cannot name
                them, the beats are not yet distinct.</li>
            <li>Write tomorrow's playtest plan: which beat do you
                MOST want watched? That beat gets a partner first
                (<a href="7.7a.html">7.7a</a>).</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"7.6a: cave greybox complete, threats placed"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Walk your whole cave entrance-to-door without bugs stopping you</li>
            <li>Name each beat as you pass through it</li>
            <li>Explain why threats go in last and geometry first</li>
            <li>Have a written playtest plan for tomorrow's audience</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — the studio checklist</li>
        <li><a href="7.5a.html">7.5a — Design a Big Cave</a> — the theory behind today's build</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.7a.html">7.7a — Level Studio II + Cross-Playtest</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="7.6a" title="7.6a Level Studio I — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
